  "articlePanels": [{"kind":"lede","html":"<p>SafetyCulture (iAuditor) charges per seat. The tier you see online may not be the tier you are quoted, and the published number changes more often than you would expect. Before you commit a field-service team to it, understand how that bill compounds, where the real cost hides, and what non-adoption quietly does to your spend.</p>"},{"kind":"takeaways","heading":"The short version","html":"<ul><li>SafetyCulture charges per seat. The more engineers you add, the more you pay, every single month. This is the heart of any honest <strong>iauditor cost uk pricing</strong> comparison.</li><li>Tiers have shifted repeatedly, so treat any <strong>safetyculture pricing uk 2026</strong> figure as a snapshot and check the live page before you decide.</li><li>The <strong>iauditor per seat cost</strong> applies whether a seat is used daily or never opened. Non-adoption does not earn a discount.</li><li>If you are asking <strong>how much is iauditor uk</strong>, the honest answer is: it depends on headcount and tier, and it climbs fast.</li><li>A full <strong>iauditor pricing breakdown</strong> has to count management time chasing inspections, compliance risk, and remediation, not just the seat rate.</li><li>The real question is not the price. It is whether your engineers will actually use it.</li></ul>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The model","heading":"Per seat, every engineer","html":"<p>SafetyCulture charges per user per month. Every engineer who needs access to iAuditor requires a paid seat. There is a free tier, but it caps the number of inspections per month and strips out functionality, which makes it unsuitable for ongoing compliance use across a team.</p><p>Beyond free, SafetyCulture has historically offered a premium plan for standard teams and an enterprise plan for larger organisations with advanced requirements. Enterprise pricing is agreed through a sales process, not a public rate card.</p><p>Pricing has changed multiple times since launch. Any figure quoted in a comparison article may be out of date by the time you read it. For current UK pricing, the only reliable source is <a href=\"https://safetyculture.com/pricing/\">safetyculture.com/pricing</a>. Check it directly before making any decision.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"10-engineer team","heading":"The cost compounds","html":"<p>The per-seat model compounds fast as a team grows. For a 10-engineer firm, the monthly bill is 10 times the per-seat rate, regardless of how many of those engineers actually complete inspections in a given month. This is where total cost of ownership splits from the headline price. Picture the realistic scenario:</p><ul><li>10 engineers on the account.</li><li>3 use iAuditor consistently.</li><li>4 use it occasionally, when reminded.</li><li>3 have not opened the app in 60 days.</li></ul><p>That pattern is common, not exceptional. You pay for 10 seats to get the compliance output of 3 to 7 active users. The bill is the same whether engineers use the tool every day or never. Add the management time chasing completions, the compliance risk from incomplete records, and the cost of remediation when an audit reveals gaps.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The free tier","heading":"What it gives, what it does not","html":"<p>SafetyCulture offers a free plan. It allows a limited number of inspections per month and a subset of features. For a sole trader running the odd inspection, the free tier may be workable.</p><p>For a firm running compliance workflows across multiple engineers, it hits its limits quickly. The restrictions vary and have changed over time, so check the current free tier limits on the SafetyCulture pricing page before assuming it covers you. The free plan is also limited on export, reporting and integrations, which are usually the very features that justify the subscription for a business user in the first place.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"A worked example","heading":"Per seat versus per firm","html":"<p>Per-seat and per-firm pricing produce wildly different numbers as a team grows. This table shows the structure, not specific SafetyCulture prices, which change, but the principle holds:</p><ul><li>1 engineer: per-seat is 1x the seat rate; Quickler is £50/mo (up to 1 eng, 3 workflows).</li><li>4 engineers: per-seat is 4x the seat rate; Quickler is £100/mo (up to 4 eng, 5 workflows).</li><li>10 engineers: per-seat is 10x the seat rate; Quickler is £140/mo (up to 12 eng, 8 workflows).</li><li>25 engineers: per-seat is 25x the seat rate; Quickler is £300/mo (up to 30 eng, 10 workflows).</li></ul><p>The per-firm model means adding an engineer does not change the monthly bill, as long as you stay within the plan limit. For a growing SME, that is a material difference in how costs scale.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The hidden cost","heading":"Seats nobody uses","html":"<p>This is the cost that never appears in a pricing comparison, and it is often the largest. The per-seat model assumes every seat is used. In reality, inspection software adoption in field-service firms is frequently lower than the buyer expects. Engineers have full days. A new tool is friction, and when the friction is high enough the tool does not get used.</p><p>So you pay for unused seats every month, the records still do not get completed, and you are paying for the privilege of that failure. Low adoption is not a moral failing. It is product-market fit. Engineers already use their phone, WhatsApp and voice notes. Forcing an app download, a login and a form is a large behaviour change, and adoption suffers in proportion.</p>"},{"kind":"default","eyebrow":"The decision","heading":"What you actually need to decide","html":"<p>The price question matters, but it sits downstream of a bigger one: will your engineers use it? A tool that costs more per month but gets used consistently is cheaper than a cheaper tool that sits idle. The compliance records that exist are worth more than the ones that do not. The smooth audit beats the one that finds gaps.</p><p>So before you weigh price, weigh adoption risk. Ask what the tool demands of an engineer on day one, and what it demands on a routine job six months in. If the answer involves an app download and a fresh login, that risk is real and belongs in your decision. This is also why Quickler charges per firm, not per seat, and why an extra engineer within the plan limit costs nothing more.</p>"},{"kind":"faq","heading":"Questions, answered","html":"<h3>How much does iAuditor cost per user in the UK?</h3><p>SafetyCulture (iAuditor) charges per seat, and the rate varies by tier and has changed over time. For accurate current figures, check their pricing page at safetyculture.com. As a broad guide, budget for a per-user monthly fee that rises significantly as headcount grows.</p><h3>Is there a free version of iAuditor?</h3><p>Yes, a free tier with limited features, typically capped on inspections per month and restricted in functionality. For ongoing compliance use across multiple engineers it is not sufficient, and a paid plan is required.</p><h3>What is the hidden cost of iAuditor for a small firm?</h3><p>Paying for seats engineers do not use. A 10-engineer team that pays for 10 seats but sees only 3 completing inspections regularly is paying full price for 70% non-adoption. The per-seat model means you pay whether the tool is used or not.</p><h3>How does Quickler compare for a 10-engineer team?</h3><p>Quickler charges per firm, not per seat. The £140/mo plan covers up to 12 engineers and 8 workflows. For a 10-engineer team on iAuditor's paid tier the monthly cost is likely to exceed this. Crucially, Quickler's cost does not rise when a new engineer joins, up to the plan limit. See our <a href=\"/pages/iauditor-alternative-uk.html\">iAuditor alternative UK</a> guide, <a href=\"/pages/how-to-replace-iauditor-uk.html\">how to replace iAuditor</a>, and <a href=\"/pages/inspection-software-uk-sme.html\">inspection software for UK SMEs</a>.</p>"}],
